WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:05.440
It's been five years since FX's beloved biker
drama Sons of Anarchy ended, and it should

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:09.920
come as no surprise that time has been kind
to the man who ended his run at the top of

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:10.420
the heap.

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:14.160
Here's what Tommy Flanagan has been doing
since playing Filip "Chibs" Telford.

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:19.380
Viewers were likely already familiar with
Tommy Flanagan's work before he slid into

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:24.240
his badass leather SAMCRO jacket for Sons
of Anarchy mainly because he'd had quite a

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:28.780
successful run in big-name projects like Braveheart,
Gladiator, and Sin City.

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:33.720
And Flanagan wasted little time booking acting
gigs in the wake of SoA's conclusion.

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:37.800
The first of those gigs came via ABC's simmering
melodrama Revenge.

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:42.520
Though Flanagan only appeared on three episodes
of Revenge, he made his presence felt as the

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:45.660
nefarious mobster-slash-arms dealer Malcolm
Black.

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:50.420
Black was a key figure on the series, and
he was also one of the few bad boys on Revenge

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:53.240
whose motivations were at least a little bit
sympathetic.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:57.880
“This is for killing my daughter.”

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:03.040
Either way, fans of the series vividly remember
Flanagan's dexterous portrayal of Black as

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:05.000
much as they do his grisly demise.

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:08.480
Tommy Flanagan has played his share of weirdos.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:13.640
That being said, the actor got a little weirder
than usual for one of his post-Sons of Anarchy

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:17.670
performances: a music video for nu metal demigods
Korn.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:22.140
The video was for the band's track "Rotting
in Vain," and found a dead-eyed, mascara-wearing

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:27.030
Flanagan portraying a dapper sort of bloke
with a penchant for, shall we say, the darker

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:28.080
side of life.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:33.260
Though the video itself is a bit tamer than
that setup would have you believe, it sees

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:38.450
Flanagan’s character kicking around an unfinished
house while huffing some mysterious gas, drinking

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:43.100
booze, and playing with ravens, snakes, and
a bevy of steam-punky devices.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:48.790
It also finds Korn playing music that sounds
decidedly like Korn which is totally cool,

00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:49.620
if that's your thing.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:53.860
As for Flanagan, he's as good as you'd expect
him to be, and he’s definitely the most

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:56.340
intriguing thing about the "Rotting in Vain"
video.

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:01.979
Like so many actors today, Tommy Flanagan
has made an appearance in the ever-expanding

00:02:01.979 --> 00:02:06.270
Marvel Cinematic Universe and most people
would agree that Flanagan's supporting turn

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:11.530
as Tullk in Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2
ranks among the more memorable of his after-Sons

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:12.560
of Anarchy gigs.

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:17.400
If you've already ventured into the goofy,
surprisingly yet heartfelt deep-space drama

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:22.170
of Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2, you'll
remember Flanagan's appearance in the film

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:24.300
as a member of the Yondu Ravager Clan.

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:29.260
Tullk was also one of the most loyal members
of Yondu's crew which is important to note

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:34.260
because one of the subplots of the film involved
an all-out mutiny among the Ravagers, and

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:38.580
those Ravagers who remained loyal to their
captain didn't fare particularly well.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:41.760
“This is mutiny!

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:43.740
This is mutiny!”

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:49.291
As it is, Flanagan's final moments in Guardians
of the Galaxy Vol. 2 also remain some of the

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:52.140
most unexpectedly touching ones in the entire
film.

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:57.400
While Charlie Hunnam's struggles with letting
go of Sons of Anarchy have been well-documented

00:02:57.410 --> 00:03:01.879
since the show went off-air, it's safe to
say that a definite bond of brotherhood ran

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:03.320
through the series' cast and crew.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:08.420
As such, some of the SAMCRO brothers have
continued to appear on screen together since

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:09.200
Sons ended.

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:15.300
That said, Tommy Flanagan's appearance opposite
his old biker boy buddy Charlie Hunnam in

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:17.880
2017's remake of Papillon was hardly a shocker.

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:23.480
The film, based on real events, finds Hunnam's
Henri "Papillon" Charrière attempting a series

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:27.880
of daring prison breaks with fellow inmate
Louis Dega, played by Rami Malek.

00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:33.100
During one of the escapes, "Papillon" is aided
in his endeavor by a savvy gent with a shotgun

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:34.660
and face full of tattoos.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:40.060
He's known simply as Masked Breton, and he's
played by Hunnam's former second-in-command

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:40.560
Flanagan.

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:45.920
After Sons of Anarchy, Tommy Flanagan joined
the ranks of actors who've appeared in Netflix

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:46.420
projects.

00:03:47.069 --> 00:03:51.660
Unfortunately, Flanagan's Netflix run lasted
a mere 10 episodes with the short-lived Wu

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:52.220
Assassins.

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:57.940
That early cancellation was likely more algorithm-driven
than anything, since the show seemed on track

00:03:57.940 --> 00:04:02.190
to become one of the more intriguing action
programs available on the platform.

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:07.659
Still, it seems not many people actually watched
those 10 episodes of Wu Assassins, which means

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:11.299
most missed out one of the great Tommy Flanagan
performances.

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:13.700
“It’s too late.”

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:18.440
The series is set in San Francisco, and finds
warrior Kai Jin who's tabbed to become the

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:22.460
last of the titular assassins and restore
balance to the streets of Chinatown.

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:28.180
Flanagan's crime boss Alec McCullough is largely
responsible for the imbalance on those streets,

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:31.580
which pits him in direct opposition to the
would-be Wu hero.

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:36.400
Flanagan clearly had a blast breaking bad
for Wu Assassins, and ultimately delivered

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:40.100
what might've become his signature villainous
turn had more people seen it.

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:45.219
They say "you can't go home again," but Tommy
Flanagan came home to his role of Chibs on

00:04:45.219 --> 00:04:50.300
the Sons of Anarchy spin-off Mayans M.C.,
and he absolutely owned the screen when he

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:50.800
did.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:55.900
While many Sons of Anarchy fans fully expected
a crossover episode when series' creator Kurt

00:04:55.909 --> 00:05:00.740
Sutter announced the spin-off, that crossover
didn't actually happen until late into the

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:02.080
show's second season.

00:05:02.389 --> 00:05:07.340
Titled "Kukulan," the episode in question
finds the Mayans crew honoring one of Jax

00:05:07.349 --> 00:05:12.050
Teller's final requests by sitting down with
the SAMCRO brothers and hammering out details

00:05:12.050 --> 00:05:13.520
about a lucrative deal.

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:17.740
As you can probably imagine, things don't
go quite go according to plan.

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:23.320
Even so, it was beyond brilliant to see Flanagan
channel his inner Chibs for the episode, and

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:27.909
we're sincerely hoping Mayans M.C. can find
another reason to bring the great Tommy Flanagan

00:05:27.909 --> 00:05:29.740
back for a future episode.

